@page "/admin"
@using System.Globalization
@inject Invitations Invitations
@inject ErrorLog ErrorLog
@inject AdminAccounts AdminAccounts

@* Who may have an account here, and what has gone wrong while running the site.
   Both stay on one page because both are the same gate — the server asks the identical question,
   `invitations.isAdministrator`, before answering either one, and a second page would only be a
   second address for the same account to reach.

   **This site is not a sign-up.** An account is what keeps somebody's characters with them and
   what opens the rulebook's own text, so who may have one is a decision rather than a form —
   and this page is where that decision is made and unmade.

   **The page decides nothing itself.** Every answer on it comes from the server, which checks on
   every single request whether the person asking may manage this list; the browser holds no
   claim, no role and no flag it could get wrong. So it is reached by its address rather than by
   a button that appears for some people — an ordinary account that finds its way here is told,
   politely, that it is not theirs, and could learn nothing by trying.

   **The failure log is read-only on purpose.** There is nothing here that clears or deletes a
   row — the server offers no such route, and adding one to this page would be inventing a
   control the server does not have. *@

<PageTitle>Who can sign in — Prowlers &amp; Paragons</PageTitle>

<h1>Who can sign in</h1>

@if (_state == ListRequest.Loaded)
{
    <Panel Title="Invited" Aside="@Counted">
        <p>Everyone here can ask for a sign-in link. Nobody else can.</p>

        <ChosenList>
            @foreach (var one in _invitations)
            {
                <ChosenRow>
                    <Body>
                        <strong>@one.Email</strong>
                        <span class="small muted">@Notes(one)</span>
                    </Body>
                    <Actions>
                        @if (one is { Removable: true, Id: { } id } && one.Email != _you)
                        {
                            <button type="button" class="btn danger"
                                    disabled="@(_working == id)"
                                    @onclick="() => Withdraw(id)">
                                @(_working == id ? "Withdrawing…" : "Withdraw")
                            </button>
                        }
                    </Actions>
                </ChosenRow>
            }
        </ChosenList>

        @if (_problem is { } problem)
        {
            <p role="alert">@problem</p>
        }
    </Panel>

    <Panel Title="Invite somebody">
        <p>
            Adding sends a sign-in link, good for three days. Re-adding does nothing.
        </p>

        <form @onsubmit="Invite" @onsubmit:preventDefault>
            <Field For="invite-email">
                <LabelContent>Their email address</LabelContent>
                <ChildContent>
                    <input id="invite-email" type="email" autocomplete="off"
                           @bind="_email" @bind:event="oninput" />
                </ChildContent>
            </Field>

            <Field>
                <LabelContent>
                    <input type="checkbox" @bind="_grantsAdmin" />
                    Let them manage this list too
                </LabelContent>
            </Field>

            <p>
                <button type="submit" class="btn" disabled="@(_inviting || _email.Length == 0)">
                    @(_inviting ? "Adding…" : "Add")
                </button>
            </p>
        </form>

        @if (_addedOutcome is { } addedOutcome)
        {
            <p role="status">@AddedMessage(addedOutcome)</p>
        }
    </Panel>

    @* The way to the demonstrations, and without it there is none.

       **They were unreachable from anywhere in the app.** The recordings and the two sample
       characters moved to `/admin/portfolio` when the front door was built, and the banner's old
       cross-link — the one thing that had ever pointed at them — was replaced in the same change
       by the Build/Rules nav. Nothing was put back, so even the one audience allowed to see the
       page had to know the address by heart. Every link into it came from inside it.

       It belongs here rather than in the banner: it is a thing to show somebody, and this is the
       page for looking after the site rather than using it. An audit found it; no test could,
       because the suite renders these pages by type and asserts what chrome an address gets once
       you are already there — never whether anybody can click their way in. *@
    <Panel Title="Showing it to somebody">
        <p>Four recorded conversations, and two finished characters.</p>
        <NavLink class="btn" href="admin/portfolio">Open the demonstrations</NavLink>
    </Panel>

    <Panel Title="What withdrawing does">
        <p>
            It ends any session that address is holding, and stops it asking for another link.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Their characters are untouched.</strong> Adding the address back restores
            everything.
        </p>
        <p class="small muted">
            Your own address is not offered here, nor the deployment's own.
        </p>
    </Panel>

    @* The players in your own campaigns, and the one number this page can move for them.

       **The cap used to be raised by hand in SQL against the live database**, which is the ops
       hazard this retires — `docs/guide/accounts-server.md` called `users.character_limit` a
       write with no gate, and it was.

       **Scoped to your campaigns and not to the whole server**, joined through `campaign_members`
       on the far side. One administrator today would not notice the difference; a second one
       would, and a privilege that only misbehaves later is the kind worth not granting.

       **A row is keyed by the address**, which is on the invitation list above anyway. Nothing
       here is told an account id — the same rule the approval screen keeps.

       **The sheets are names and times, never a character.** A GM reads a member's actual sheet
       on the approval screen, where it is one that member sent them. *@
    <Panel Title="Who plays in your campaigns" Aside="@PlayersCounted">
        @* **An empty list and a list that could not be read are two sentences**, which is the
           rule a player's sheets already followed one level down and this panel did not: every
           non-2xx arrived as "not for you", the page turned anything that was not Loaded into an
           empty list, and a single 500 from storage told a GM their games were empty. *@
        @if (_accountsState is not AdminAccountsRequest.Loaded)
        {
            <p role="alert">
                The players could not be read just now. Nothing has changed — reload and try
                again.
            </p>
        }
        else if (_accounts.Count == 0)
        {
            <p>Nobody is in one of your campaigns yet. Anyone who joins one appears here.</p>
        }
        else
        {
            <p>
                How many characters each of them may keep. Lowering it never touches what they
                already have — it only stops the next one.
            </p>

            <ChosenList>
                @for (var i = 0; i < _accounts.Count; i++)
                {
                    var one = _accounts[i];
                    var capId = $"cap-{i}";
                    var sheetsId = $"sheets-{i}";
                    var open = _showing == one.Email;

                    <ChosenRow>
                        <Body>
                            <strong>@(one.DisplayName ?? one.Email)</strong>
                            <span class="small muted">@one.Email · @Held(one)</span>

                            @* **The address is in the label, clipped**, because twenty rows of
                               "Characters they may keep" are twenty controls a screen reader
                               cannot tell apart — the accessible name is the whole of what is
                               announced on arriving at one, and a row's identity is above it on
                               screen where nothing links the two. Same `sr-only` idiom the
                               descriptions and the empty-cell notes use.

                               `min`/`max` are the browser's affordance and not a rule this page
                               decides — `AccountsContractTests` pins the `max` to
                               `MAX_CHARACTER_LIMIT` in `worker/adminAccounts.js`, so the copy
                               cannot drift from the bound the server actually enforces. *@
                            <Field For="@capId">
                                <LabelContent>
                                    Characters they may keep<span class="sr-only"> — @one.Email</span>
                                </LabelContent>
                                <ChildContent>
                                    <input id="@capId" type="number" min="0" max="500"
                                           value="@Draft(one)"
                                           @oninput="e => Redraft(one, e.Value)" />
                                </ChildContent>
                            </Field>

                            @* aria-controls only while the list is really rendered: naming an
                               element inside an @if unconditionally leaves a dangling IDREF,
                               which is the bug the budget disclosure shipped once. aria-expanded
                               is a string, because Blazor renders a true bool as `=""`, which is
                               invalid ARIA that reads as *not* expanded. *@
                            <button type="button" class="btn small"
                                    aria-expanded="@(open ? "true" : "false")"
                                    aria-controls="@(open ? sheetsId : null)"
                                    @onclick="() => ShowSheets(one.Email)">
                                @(open ? "Hide their sheets" : "Their sheets")
                            </button>

                            @if (open)
                            {
                                <div id="@sheetsId">
                                    @if (_sheets.Count == 0)
                                    {
                                        <p class="small muted">@EmptySheets</p>
                                    }
                                    else
                                    {
                                        <ul>
                                            @foreach (var sheet in _sheets)
                                            {
                                                <li>
                                                    @sheet.Label
                                                    <span class="small muted">@Describe(sheet)</span>
                                                </li>
                                            }
                                        </ul>
                                    }
                                </div>
                            }
                        </Body>
                        <Actions>
                            <button type="button" class="btn"
                                    disabled="@(!Changed(one) || _capping is not null)"
                                    @onclick="() => Cap(one)">
                                @(_capping == one.Email ? "Saving…" : "Save")
                            </button>
                        </Actions>
                    </ChosenRow>
                }
            </ChosenList>
        }

        @if (_capProblem is { } capProblem)
        {
            <p role="alert">@capProblem</p>
        }
    </Panel>

    <Panel Title="What has gone wrong" Aside="@ErrorLogCounted">
        @if (_errorRows.Count == 0)
        {
            <p>Nothing has failed here in the last month.</p>
        }
        else
        {
            <p>
                One row per failure, counting every occurrence. No addresses, no characters.
            </p>

            <ChosenList>
                @foreach (var row in _errorRows)
                {
                    <ChosenRow>
                        <Body>
                            <strong>@CategoryLabel(row.Category)</strong>
                            <span class="small muted">@row.Route · @row.Kind</span>
                            @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(row.Detail))
                            {
                                <p class="small muted">@row.Detail</p>
                            }
                            <p class="small muted">
                                @Occurred(row) Quote reference @row.Reference to report it.
                            </p>
                        </Body>
                    </ChosenRow>
                }
            </ChosenList>
        }
    </Panel>
}
else if (_state is ListRequest.NotSignedIn or ListRequest.Unavailable or ListRequest.NotForYou)
{
    <GateRefusal State="_state" />
}
else
{
    <Panel Title="Who can sign in">
        <p>Looking…</p>
    </Panel>
}

@code {
    private ListRequest? _state;
    private IReadOnlyList<Invitation> _invitations = [];
    private string? _you;
    private IReadOnlyList<ErrorLogRow> _errorRows = [];

    private string _email = "";
    private bool _grantsAdmin;
    private bool _inviting;
    private string? _added;
    private InvitationAddResult? _addedOutcome;
    private string? _problem;

    /// <summary>The accounts panel's own refusal, kept apart from the invitation list's.</summary>
    private string? _capProblem;

    /// <summary>The id being withdrawn, so one button reads as busy rather than all of them.</summary>
    private string? _working;

    private string Counted =>
        _invitations.Count == 1 ? "1 address" : $"{_invitations.Count} addresses";

    private IReadOnlyList<ManagedAccount> _accounts = [];

    /// <summary>
    /// What asking for them did — kept, rather than collapsed into an empty list.
    ///
    /// <para><b>An empty list is a fact about your campaigns and a refusal is a fact about the
    /// server</b>, and a panel holding only the first cannot tell them apart. Loaded until the
    /// first answer, because nothing draws this panel before then.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private AdminAccountsRequest _accountsState = AdminAccountsRequest.Loaded;

    /// <summary>
    /// What is typed into each row's box, keyed by the address the row is keyed by.
    ///
    /// <para><b>The text, not a parsed number.</b> A box holding <c>--</c> or nothing at all has
    /// no number in it, and a dictionary of ints has no way to say so — the last thing that
    /// parsed stayed, Save stayed live against it, and a click wrote a figure the box was not
    /// showing. Keeping the text also stops a re-render putting a parsed value back over what
    /// somebody is half-way through typing.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private readonly Dictionary<string, string> _drafts = new(StringComparer.Ordinal);

    /// <summary>The address whose cap is being written, so one button reads as busy, not all.</summary>
    private string? _capping;

    /// <summary>Whose sheets are open, or null. One at a time — this is a disclosure, not a table.</summary>
    private string? _showing;

    private IReadOnlyList<ManagedCharacter> _sheets = [];

    /// <summary>
    /// How many times a disclosure has been worked, so a late answer knows it is late.
    ///
    /// <para>See <see cref="ShowSheets"/>. A sequence number rather than a "busy" flag, because
    /// the second click must open the second row at once rather than being ignored — the
    /// stale reply is the thing to drop, not the new request.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private int _asked;

    /// <summary>
    /// The sentence for a disclosure that came back with nothing, which is two different facts.
    ///
    /// <para>An account that holds no characters, and an account whose list could not be read —
    /// and a panel that said "none" for the second would be telling somebody their player has
    /// built nothing on the strength of a failed request.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string _emptySheets = "";

    private string EmptySheets => _emptySheets;

    private string PlayersCounted =>
        _accountsState is not AdminAccountsRequest.Loaded ? "not read"
        : _accounts.Count == 1 ? "1 player"
        : $"{_accounts.Count} players";

    /// <summary>What a row says about the number beside it, in the same "N of M" the roster uses.</summary>
    private static string Held(ManagedAccount one) =>
        $"{one.CharacterCount} of {one.CharacterLimit} characters";

    /// <summary>What is in the box: what was typed there, or the server's number until it is.</summary>
    private string Draft(ManagedAccount one) =>
        _drafts.TryGetValue(one.Email, out var typed)
            ? typed
            : one.CharacterLimit.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);

    /// <summary>
    /// The number in the box, or null because what is in it is not one.
    ///
    /// <para>An empty box, a lone <c>-</c> part-way through typing, <c>1e5</c> — a
    /// <c>type="number"</c> input hands its own text over and none of those is an integer. Null
    /// is the answer, and everything that would act on the box refuses on it.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private int? Number(ManagedAccount one) =>
        int.TryParse(Draft(one), NumberStyles.Integer, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, out var n)
            ? n
            : null;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether there is a different number in the box from the one the server holds.
    ///
    /// <para><b>A box with no number in it is not a change</b>, which is what puts Save out of
    /// reach for it. It used to be: the last text that parsed stayed in the draft, so clearing a
    /// box left Save live against a number nothing on screen was showing, and pressing it wrote
    /// that number.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private bool Changed(ManagedAccount one) =>
        Number(one) is { } number && number != one.CharacterLimit;

    /// <summary>
    /// Take what was typed, whatever it is.
    ///
    /// <para><b>The range is not checked here</b> — that is the server's, and a second copy of it
    /// would be a rule this page made up. Nor is it parsed here: see <see cref="Number"/>.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private void Redraft(ManagedAccount one, object? typed) =>
        _drafts[one.Email] = typed?.ToString() ?? "";

    private string ErrorLogCounted =>
        _errorRows.Count == 0 ? "none recorded"
        : _errorRows.Count == 1 ? "1 recorded"
        : $"{_errorRows.Count} recorded";

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync() => await Reload();

    private async Task Reload()
    {
        var answer = await Invitations.ListAsync();

        _state = answer.Result;
        _invitations = answer.Invitations;
        _you = answer.You;

        // Asked for only once this account is known to manage the list — the server gates the
        // two identically, but there is no reason to make the call before the first answer says
        // it could possibly succeed.
        _errorRows = _state == ListRequest.Loaded
            ? (await ErrorLog.ListAsync()) is { Result: ErrorLogRequest.Loaded } log
                ? log.Rows
                : []
            : [];

        // Asked for on the same terms as the failure log, and for the same reason: the server
        // gates all three identically, so there is nothing to gain by calling before the first
        // answer says it could possibly succeed.
        var players = _state == ListRequest.Loaded
            ? await AdminAccounts.ListAsync()
            : new AdminAccountsView(AdminAccountsRequest.Loaded, []);

        _accountsState = players.Result;
        _accounts = players.Accounts;

        // **Anything typed against a row that is no longer there goes with it — and nothing
        // else.** A draft kept across a reload would sit in a box beside somebody else's number
        // after the list moved; but clearing every one of them threw away an edit somebody was
        // part-way through in another row, silently, on every Save, Invite and Withdraw on this
        // page. A row that came back with its cap already set to what was typed reads as
        // unchanged anyway, so the saved row needs no clearing to go quiet.
        var stillHere = _accounts.Select(a => a.Email).ToHashSet(StringComparer.Ordinal);

        foreach (var gone in _drafts.Keys.Where(k => !stillHere.Contains(k)).ToList())
        {
            _drafts.Remove(gone);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Set one player's cap, and say which of the three things happened.
    ///
    /// <para>The list is re-read afterwards rather than the row being patched in place: the count
    /// beside the cap is the server's answer about that account, and a page that trusted its own
    /// arithmetic there would be showing a figure nobody computed.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task Cap(ManagedAccount one)
    {
        // The button is dead without a number in the box, so this is the belt to that brace:
        // nothing may be sent that is not what the box is showing.
        if (Number(one) is not { } wanted) return;

        _capping = one.Email;
        _capProblem = null;

        var outcome = await AdminAccounts.SetCharacterLimitAsync(one.Email, wanted);

        _capProblem = outcome.Result switch
        {
            CapChange.Refused => "That was not saved. A cap is a whole number from 0 to 500.",
            CapChange.NotThere =>
                "That was not saved. That account is no longer in one of your campaigns.",
            CapChange.Unavailable => "That was not saved, and nothing said why. Reload and retry.",
            _ => null,
        };

        await Reload();
        _capping = null;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Open one player's sheets, or shut the one that is open.
    ///
    /// <para>Read when it is opened rather than with the list, because a list of twenty players
    /// would otherwise be twenty requests for something nobody has asked to see.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>An answer nobody is waiting for any more is dropped, and that is the whole of
    /// <see cref="_asked"/>.</b> There is one <c>_sheets</c> for the one open disclosure, so
    /// opening a second row while the first is still in flight let the first row's answer land
    /// underneath the second row's name — a GM reading one player's characters attributed to
    /// another. The counter moves on every open and every shut, and a reply that does not carry
    /// the current one is stale by construction. Re-comparing the address would not do: opening a
    /// row, shutting it and opening it again is the same address twice.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ShowSheets(string email)
    {
        var mine = ++_asked;

        if (_showing == email)
        {
            _showing = null;
            _sheets = [];

            return;
        }

        _showing = email;

        // Nothing of the row that was open survives into this one, so a slow answer is never a
        // moment of somebody else's list under this player's name.
        _sheets = [];
        _emptySheets = "Reading their sheets…";

        var held = await AdminAccounts.HeldByAsync(email);

        if (mine != _asked) return;

        _sheets = held.Characters;
        _emptySheets = held.Result == AdminAccountsRequest.Loaded
            ? "Nothing saved to their account yet."
            : "Their sheets could not be read just now.";
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// What a sheet says beyond its name: the tier it is built to, its spend, and when it moved.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Every one of the three is drawn only when there is one</b>, which is the front
    /// door's rule and the roster's: a row written before the index columns existed has no tier
    /// and no figure, and the engine refuses to price an incomplete selection rather than guessing
    /// — so "no figure" is an answer rather than a gap. <b>A tier is printed as the id it is</b>,
    /// the same choice the diff makes: this page has not read the rules and "Unnamed" would tell a
    /// reader less than the id does.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private static string Describe(ManagedCharacter sheet)
    {
        var parts = new List<string>();

        if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(sheet.TierId)) parts.Add(sheet.TierId);
        if (sheet.Spent is { } spent) parts.Add($"{spent} HP");
        if (Ages.Since(sheet.UpdatedAt, DateTimeOffset.UtcNow) is { } age) parts.Add(age);

        return string.Join(" · ", parts);
    }

    /// <summary>A plain name for the subsystem, matching the words <c>SignIn.razor</c> uses for
    /// the same four categories — a visitor who reports a failure and whoever reads this row
    /// should be able to recognise it as the same thing.</summary>
    private static string CategoryLabel(FailureCategory category) => category switch
    {
        FailureCategory.Mail => "sending mail",
        FailureCategory.Storage => "saving what it was asked to save",
        FailureCategory.Configuration => "how this deployment is set up",
        _ => "something unclassified",
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// How often, and — the reason this exists — whether it is still happening.
    ///
    /// <para>A row here is a fact about the last month, not about right now: the one row this
    /// site has ever produced was an outage that ended days before anybody read the table. A
    /// count on its own reads as an ongoing fire; a count next to "hasn't happened again" does
    /// not.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private static string Occurred(ErrorLogRow row)
    {
        var times = row.Occurrences == 1 ? "Happened once" : $"Happened {row.Occurrences} times";
        var last = DateTimeOffset.FromUnixTimeMilliseconds(row.LastAt);
        var quiet = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow - last > TimeSpan.FromHours(24);

        return quiet
            ? $"{times}, most recently {When(last)}, and not again since."
            : $"{times}, most recently {When(last)}.";
    }

    private static string When(DateTimeOffset at) => at.ToLocalTime().ToString("d MMM, HH:mm");

    private async Task Invite()
    {
        _inviting = true;
        _problem = null;
        _added = null;
        _addedOutcome = null;

        var email = _email.Trim();
        var outcome = await Invitations.AddAsync(email, _grantsAdmin);

        if (outcome == InvitationAddResult.Refused)
        {
            // The server's own refusals are a malformed address and nothing else, so this is the
            // one sentence worth saying. It deliberately does not advise trying again: the same
            // address would be refused the same way.
            _problem = "That was not added. Check it is an email address.";
        }
        else
        {
            _added = email;
            _addedOutcome = outcome;
            _email = "";
            _grantsAdmin = false;
            await Reload();
        }

        _inviting = false;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The one sentence for whichever of the three ways adding an address can end.
    ///
    /// <para>All three are a kind of success — the server never refuses an address it has
    /// already accepted the shape of — so none of them is <see cref="_problem"/>; they differ
    /// only in what the reader now needs to do, if anything.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string AddedMessage(InvitationAddResult outcome) => outcome switch
    {
        InvitationAddResult.Added => $"{_added} has been sent a link, and can sign in now.",
        InvitationAddResult.AddedButNotMailed =>
            $"{_added} can sign in now, but the link could not be sent — let them know another way.",
        InvitationAddResult.AlreadyAllowed => $"{_added} could already sign in.",
        _ => "",
    };

    private async Task Withdraw(string id)
    {
        _working = id;
        _problem = null;
        _added = null;

        if (!await Invitations.RemoveAsync(id))
        {
            _problem = "That was not withdrawn. Reload the page and try again.";
        }

        await Reload();
        _working = null;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// What is true of one address beyond the address itself.
    ///
    /// <para>Whether it has been used matters on this site more than it would on most: the mail
    /// that carries a sign-in link has already failed here once, silently, so "invited and never
    /// signed in" is a state somebody looking at this page needs to be able to see.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string Notes(Invitation one)
    {
        var notes = new List<string>();

        if (one.GrantsAdmin) notes.Add("manages this list");
        if (!one.Removable) notes.Add("set on the deployment");
        if (one.Email == _you) notes.Add("you");
        notes.Add(one.HasSignedIn ? "has signed in" : "has not signed in yet");

        return string.Join(" · ", notes);
    }
}
